1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:07,167
Before maintenance, an engineer drains a Kubernetes node, and every pod on it must fit somewhere else.

2
00:00:07,167 --> 00:00:13,367
Each node has limited CPU, memory and pod slots, and some pods may only run on certain nodes.

3
00:00:13,367 --> 00:00:17,733
QPlace checks whether that is possible before anything is evicted.

4
00:00:18,733 --> 00:00:20,200
Here is a small trap.

5
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:27,133
Greedy placement handles the biggest pod first and puts batch on the first node that fits, node-a.

6
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:29,033
That leaves 500 millicores.

7
00:00:29,033 --> 00:00:35,167
The indexer pod needs an SSD node, and node-a is the only one, so it has nowhere to go.

8
00:00:35,167 --> 00:00:37,500
Greedy places one pod out of two.

9
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:39,767
A plan does exist.

10
00:00:39,767 --> 00:00:46,567
Our exact branch-and-bound solver puts batch on node-b and indexer on node-a, so both pods fit.

11
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:56,033
An independent verifier checks every assignment against the cluster model: eligibility, CPU, memory and pod slots, added up per node.

12
00:00:57,033 --> 00:01:03,167
For the quantum experiment, each allowed pod-to-node choice becomes one qubit, three in total.

13
00:01:03,167 --> 00:01:11,267
The QUBO rewards every placed pod and penalizes breaking a rule: putting one pod in two places, or overloading node-a's CPU.

14
00:01:11,267 --> 00:01:19,167
Before any quantum run, we check every bitstring to prove the lowest-energy states are exactly the best valid placements.

15
00:01:19,167 --> 00:01:21,767
QAOA at depth two then prepares a state,

16
00:01:21,767 --> 00:01:25,100
and each measured bitstring decodes to a placement.

17
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:35,300
We ran the project on Qollab with a single job per run.

18
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:40,733
On the built-in simulator, 84 of 256 shots gave the complete placement.

19
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:45,567
On IonQ's simulator with the Aria 1 noise model, 83 of 256 did.

20
00:01:45,567 --> 00:01:50,067
Random guessing would hit it only 12.5 percent of the time.

21
00:01:50,067 --> 00:01:56,100
Every sample was decoded and checked; invalid ones are counted, never repaired.

22
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:03,633
The classical solver is still faster and proves optimality, so we claim no quantum advantage.

23
00:02:03,633 --> 00:02:12,367
What QPlace shows is a working hybrid pipeline: from a cluster snapshot, to a verified quantum sample, with honest baselines.

24
00:02:12,367 --> 00:02:16,033
It is read-only, and the demo instance is synthetic.

25
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:21,300
The code is open source at github dot com slash edeediong slash qplace-k8s.

26
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:24,367
This effort is supported by Qollab and IonQ.

